        "writtenLesson": "Act I: The Cold Open — Death by a Thousand Subscriptions\n\nDigital creators love to brag: \"My digital product business has 99% profit margins because software has zero cost of goods sold!\"\n\nThis is an illusion. While you do not have physical manufacturing costs, a digital product funnel requires a substantial underlying infrastructure to operate:\n- Funnel Builder / Hosting: $29 to $99/mo\n- Email Marketing Service: $20 to $50/mo\n- Custom Domain & Workspace: $12/mo\n- Vimeo / Video Hosting: $15/mo\n- Form & Tracking Software: $29/mo\n\nBefore you make a single sale, you have $100 to $200 in monthly fixed SaaS overhead.\nIf you sell 10 copies of a $17 product, your entire gross revenue ($170) has been completely devoured by your software stack before paying a single cent for ads!\n\nIn the experiment, Andre Simmons accounted for these operational frictions, which is why $623 in revenue yielded only $46 in final profit.\n\nAct II: The Variable Fee Traps\n\nBeyond fixed software subscriptions, watch for these silent percentage leaks:\n1. International Surcharges:\n   When selling globally, Stripe applies an additional 1.5% cross-border fee plus a 1% currency conversion fee if the buyer purchases in a foreign denomination.\n2. Digital Goods Sales Tax & VAT:\n   In the European Union, UK, and several US states, digital products are legally subject to Value Added Tax (VAT) or sales tax. If your checkout is not configured to collect tax on top of the $17 price, you must pay that tax out of your own pocket, eroding another 10% to 20% of your gross receipt!\n3. Refund Processing Penalties:\n   When you issue a refund in Stripe, Stripe does *not* return the $0.30 fixed fee or the 2.9% variable fee they took on the original transaction. You lose money every time a refund occurs.\n\nAct III: Building the Forensic Expense Ledger\n\nTo know your real numbers, classify expenses into three strict buckets:\n- Direct Variable Costs: Ad spend, merchant processing fees, currency spreads.\n- Direct Fixed Operating Costs: Monthly software, domain renewals, hosting.\n- Capital Reserves: Refund buffer (5% of gross) and tax liability reserve.\n\nAct IV: Strategic Implementation Protocols and Battle Exercises\n\nExecute the \"SaaS Pruning Exercise\":\n\nStep 1: Open Your Credit Card Statement\nAudit every recurring software charge from the last 60 days.\n\nStep 2: Eliminate Redundant Tools\nAre you paying for an expensive all-in-one funnel platform when a clean, low-cost static site generator or unified checkout tool can execute the same job for a fraction of the cost?\n\nStep 3: Calculate Your Monthly Operational Break-Even Units\nDivide your total monthly fixed software overhead by your net margin per unit:\nBreak-Even Units = Total Monthly SaaS ÷ $15.50.\nKnow exactly how many units you must sell every month just to keep the lights on before ads."
